WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.970
One morning, in the very middle of the most crowded act of worship on earth, a group of men who called themselves pilgrims drew their swords.

2
00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:15.780
When they left, they left behind a Kaaba with its door torn off, a well stuffed with corpses, and an empty hollow in a wall.

3
00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:23.910
That stone, from the heart of the Islamic world, would stand for twenty-one years in the wall of another mosque, a thousand kilometres away.

4
00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:41.200
The coastline of eastern Arabia — that thin band of the Persian Gulf wedged between sand and salt — is a place that has rarely attracted the interest of great empires.

5
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:49.010
Date groves, springs of fresh water, a few harbours where pearl divers lived, and beyond them, emptiness stretching inland.

6
00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:59.220
At the end of the ninth century, something took shape in that emptiness that the caliphal court in Baghdad never saw coming: a state that minted its own coinage, enforced its own law,

7
00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:03.510
and carried its own calendar and its own expectation of a saviour.

8
00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:08.280
The roots of the story reach back to the barren villages around Kufa.

9
00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:13.690
In the eight seventies, travelling missionaries of the Ismaili call were moving through this region.

10
00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:25.980
What they preached was profoundly appealing to peasants crushed under heavy taxes and to the bedouin at the desert's edge: the present order is temporary, oppression will not last forever, the awaited imam is near,

11
00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:30.750
and when he comes, everything will be turned upside down.

12
00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:35.680
The man who organised this call in the region was named Hamdan Qarmat.

13
00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:40.450
The movement took its name from his: they came to be called the Qarmatians.

14
00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:46.820
Hamdan Qarmat vanished from the historical record around nine oh six, but the network he built lived on.

15
00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:51.910
The furthest and most durable branch of that network took root in Bahrayn.

16
00:01:51.910 --> 00:02:00.840
This Bahrayn is not today's island state; it is the broad coastal region stretching from Qatif to al-Ahsa, which the Arabs of that age called Bahrayn.

17
00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:05.290
The missionary sent to the region was named Abu Sa'id al-Jannabi.

18
00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:10.380
His title came from the town of Jannaba in Iran, where his family originated.

19
00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:19.470
Abu Sa'id began his work disguised as a flour merchant, won over the tribes one by one, and in eight ninety-nine seized al-Ahsa and made it his capital.

20
00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:29.360
That same year, a great rupture tore through the movement: the leadership in Syria proclaimed that the awaited imam had now appeared — and that this man was Ubayd Allah al-Mahdi,

21
00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.170
who would later found the Fatimid dynasty in North Africa.

22
00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:36.980
The Qarmatians of Bahrayn refused to accept the claim.

23
00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:42.550
As far as they were concerned, the awaited saviour had still not come, and come he would.

24
00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:53.720
That refusal was a fracture that would determine a great deal in the years ahead: they now owed allegiance neither to the Sunni caliphate in Baghdad nor to the Ismaili caliphate in North Africa.

25
00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:55.930
To no one.

26
00:02:55.930 --> 00:03:03.580
Abu Sa'id was assassinated in nine thirteen; the sources report that he died in his bathhouse, at the hand of a servant.

27
00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:05.950
Rule passed to his sons.

28
00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:11.680
What made this state truly extraordinary was less its capacity for war than its internal order.

29
00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:21.090
A century later, in ten fifty-one, the Persian traveller Nasir-i Khusraw passed through al-Ahsa and described what he saw with astonishment.

30
00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:24.580
No taxes were levied on the people of the city.

31
00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:28.710
Anyone in difficulty was given an interest-free loan.

32
00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:37.800
Local trade was conducted not in gold or silver but with tokens made of lead; these tokens were valid only inside the city, which meant that wealth could not seep out.

33
00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:41.930
The state's mills ground the people's grain free of charge.

34
00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:45.740
Houses that fell into ruin were repaired at public expense.

35
00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:56.430
Government did not rest in the hands of a single ruler: a council of six, drawn from the descendants of Abu Sa'id, took the decisions, and in disputes an advisory assembly stepped in.

36
00:03:56.430 --> 00:04:02.160
There was no mosque in the city where the Friday prayer was held; the call to prayer was not sounded.

37
00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:08.370
The saviour had not come, and so, in their view, the older forms of worship were suspended.

38
00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:14.420
It is easy to read this picture as a medieval utopia, but the foundation beneath it was far darker.

39
00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:24.310
According to the same account by Nasir-i Khusraw, those vast date and grain estates around al-Ahsa and Qatif were worked by the labour of some thirty thousand Black slaves.

40
00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:34.840
The absence of taxes, the interest-free credit, the free milling — all of it belonged to the community of property owners; the hands that filled that community's common treasury owned nothing at all.

41
00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:41.690
This state built in the desert was an egalitarian machine for its own members and a merciless one for everyone outside.

42
00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:45.340
And there was a second source of revenue: the raid.

43
00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:54.430
Because the Qarmatians regarded themselves as standing outside the existing order, they treated that order's caravans, cities and pilgrims as legitimate targets.

44
00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:59.520
The storehouses of al-Ahsa were also filled with plunder taken on the roads of Iraq.

45
00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:04.770
And in time, these raids would move far beyond an ordinary economy of pillage.

46
00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:11.140
To think of the Qarmatians merely as a desert state of brigands is not enough to explain what happened in nine thirty.

47
00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:15.110
What brought them to that point was a belief about time itself.

48
00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:19.080
This belief held that history moved in closed cycles.

49
00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:23.370
Every great prophet had his era, and every era had its end.

50
00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:33.420
The order Muhammad had brought would one day run out its term as well; then the awaited redeemer would come, what was sealed would be opened, and outward law would give way to inner truth.

51
00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:40.270
With the break of eight ninety-nine, the community in Bahrayn had chosen to keep that expectation alive.

52
00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:47.760
For them, in other words, the question was not whether to obey religious law, but whether that law still held at all.

53
00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:52.370
According to the sources, this reckoning drew support from the heavens as well:

54
00:05:52.370 --> 00:06:00.820
the great conjunction of stars that astrologers placed at the turn of the century was read as the date when the age of Islam would close and a new age would open.

55
00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:08.790
The man who showed how destructive that expectation could be is Abu Tahir Sulayman al-Jannabi, who took power in nine twenty-three.

56
00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:16.600
He was one of the sons of Abu Sa'id; he pushed his elder brother aside, seized the leadership, and entered Basra that same year.

57
00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:22.010
In nine twenty-seven he sacked Kufa and routed the caliphal army sent against him.

58
00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:30.620
In nine twenty-eight he advanced to the very threshold of Baghdad; he could not enter the capital, but he ranged across most of Iraq and plundered it.

59
00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:41.470
Before all these raids, back in nine hundred and six, the pilgrim caravan returning from Mecca had been ambushed, and by the figure the sources give, close to twenty thousand pilgrims were put to the sword.

60
00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:51.040
The Qarmatians, then, were not a community that shrank from laying hands on the sacred roads; on the contrary, laying hands on those roads was itself their message.

61
00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:57.250
To grasp where this mindset could lead, we need to look one year ahead of Mecca, to nine thirty-one.

62
00:06:57.250 --> 00:07:03.140
Because that was the year the Qarmatian state came face to face with the logical conclusion of its own faith.

63
00:07:03.140 --> 00:07:12.070
In nine thirty-one Abu Tahir gave his allegiance, as to the awaited redeemer, to a young man from Isfahan — Abu'l-Fadl al-Isfahani,

64
00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:17.800
who counted himself a descendant of the Sasanian kings — and handed the government over to him.

65
00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:23.210
The one who for long years had been called "the one who is to come" had come.

66
00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:28.620
The result was a devastation that not even the community's own leaders had reckoned with.

67
00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:31.630
The new mahdi abolished the whole of religious law.

68
00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:35.280
The obligations of prayer and fasting fell away.

69
00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:37.330
Books were burned.

70
00:07:37.330 --> 00:07:44.340
The prophets were openly cursed — not Muhammad alone, but an entire line stretching from Abraham to Jesus.

71
00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:52.950
The man rejected the supremacy of the Arabs, began to exalt the legacy of the Persian kings, and had a number of the Qarmatian notables executed.

72
00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:57.720
This was an age of salvation that began with the blood of its own leaders.

73
00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:01.050
Abu Tahir endured it for eighty days.

74
00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:07.100
Then he declared that the mahdi he had raised to the throne with his own hands was an impostor, and had him killed.

75
00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:15.710
The Qarmatian state never fully recovered from those eighty days; Abu Tahir's spiritual authority within the community was gravely wounded,

76
00:08:15.710 --> 00:08:20.640
and the movement's branches in other regions lost their trust in the center at Bahrayn.

77
00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:26.690
This episode shows the mental climate in which what happened at Mecca a year earlier was possible.

78
00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:34.340
Because for someone who genuinely believes the old order has run out its term, the holiest object of that order is no longer untouchable.

79
00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:36.550
It is a portable thing.

80
00:08:36.550 --> 00:08:38.600
It can be bargained over.

81
00:08:38.600 --> 00:08:45.930
It can even be prised from its place and set in the mosque of another city, with the expectation that people will turn toward it there.

82
00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:49.100
That is precisely what Abu Tahir attempted.

83
00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:54.350
And the day he attempted it was the most shattering morning in the tenth-century Islamic world.

84
00:08:54.350 --> 00:09:01.680
The eighth day of Dhu al-Hijjah in the year three hundred and seventeen — the eleventh of January, nine thirty.

85
00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:05.650
It was the most crowded moment of the pilgrimage season.

86
00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:13.940
The pilgrims were in a state of ritual consecration; they wore nothing but two seamless pieces of white cloth, and they were forbidden to carry weapons.

87
00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:17.270
Most were preparing to set out for Mina the next day.

88
00:09:17.270 --> 00:09:25.400
The city was filled with people who had come from every corner of the world: from Khurasan, from Egypt, from al-Andalus, from Yemen.

89
00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:30.810
When Abu Tahir arrived at the city gate with his horsemen, he did not force his way in.

90
00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:36.860
He said that every Muslim had the right to enter this city, and gave his assurance that he would harm no one.

91
00:09:36.860 --> 00:09:42.430
Mecca's resistance was fragile, the assurance was accepted, and the gates were opened.

92
00:09:42.430 --> 00:09:44.640
Then the swords were drawn.

93
00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:49.890
The sources recount the days that followed with differing details, but with the same horror.

94
00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:56.900
Qarmatian cavalry rode through the courtyard of the Sacred Mosque, into the consecrated and defenseless crowd.

95
00:09:56.900 --> 00:10:04.070
It is reported that as they killed, they recited verses of the Qur'an, mocking their victims with the words of their own scripture.

96
00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:09.160
At night they left the city for their camps outside, and in the morning they came back in.

97
00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:13.290
This went on for eight days; in some accounts, eleven.

98
00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:22.060
The sources do not agree on the number of the dead; some say thousands within the bounds of the sanctuary, others put the total as high as thirty thousand.

99
00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:29.230
This much is certain: the bodies went unburied for days, left lying in the streets and in the courtyard.

100
00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:32.080
Then something heavier still was done.

101
00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:35.250
Some of the dead were thrown into the well of Zamzam.

102
00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:41.300
Mecca's lifeblood, the well whose water pilgrims drank as a healing, was filled with corpses.

103
00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:44.950
This was not a military act; it was a message.

104
00:10:44.950 --> 00:10:49.400
A message that said: nothing here is sacred.

105
00:10:49.400 --> 00:10:52.090
The Kaaba itself became a target.

106
00:10:52.090 --> 00:10:57.180
Its covering was pulled down, cut into pieces and divided up as spoils.

107
00:10:57.180 --> 00:11:00.030
Its door was torn from its frame.

108
00:11:00.030 --> 00:11:06.080
It is recorded that the Kaaba was stained with the blood of slaughtered pilgrims, and that the building was set alight.

109
00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:15.970
The scale of the plunder is described in such terms that more than fifty camels were said to be needed to carry it away; modern historians regard that figure as an exaggeration.

110
00:11:15.970 --> 00:11:22.020
The governor of Mecca, Muhammad ibn Isma'il, was killed while trying to defend the city.

111
00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:29.990
A few things were saved: the Station of Abraham was smuggled out of the city, and the golden waterspout on the roof remained in place.

112
00:11:29.990 --> 00:11:33.320
But what Abu Tahir really wanted was something else.

113
00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:41.290
In the eastern corner of the Kaaba, set into the wall at a height just below that of a man, was a dark-coloured stone.

114
00:11:41.290 --> 00:11:43.020
The Black Stone.

115
00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:53.710
In Islamic tradition it is held to have been placed there from the time the Kaaba was built; it marks the starting point of each circuit during the circumambulation, and it is customary to kiss it,

116
00:11:53.710 --> 00:11:57.200
or to salute it from a distance.

117
00:11:57.200 --> 00:12:01.970
The one physical object believed to have been touched by the Prophet's hand.

118
00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:13.620
According to the account, when the people of Mecca invoked the sanctity of the Kaaba against him, Abu Tahir answered that this house did not belong to God, and that God would not choose a house for Himself.

119
00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:17.750
As the stone was prised from the wall with pick and iron, it broke.

120
00:12:17.750 --> 00:12:25.400
The piece was already cracked, and under this assault it came apart into several fragments; the sources speak of seven pieces.

121
00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:33.050
The fragments were gathered up, loaded onto camels, and the caravan set out eastward, towards Hajar.

122
00:12:33.050 --> 00:12:36.060
What was left behind was an empty hollow.

123
00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:41.790
In the corner of the Kaaba, where the stone had been torn out, a cavity opening into the wall.

124
00:12:41.790 --> 00:12:48.960
The point where, eight days earlier, people from the four corners of the world had queued and reached out to touch, was now a wound.

125
00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:51.650
Abu Tahir's plan was plain.

126
00:12:51.650 --> 00:12:54.980
He installed the stone in the mosque of his own country.

127
00:12:54.980 --> 00:13:00.870
His intention was to redirect the pilgrimage, to tear people away from Mecca and draw them to Hajar.

128
00:13:00.870 --> 00:13:05.800
He believed that as long as he held the stone, he would hold its sanctity too.

129
00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:09.770
This was the beginning of a delusion that would last twenty-one years.

130
00:13:09.770 --> 00:13:14.060
When the news reached Baghdad, al-Muqtadir sat on the caliph's throne.

131
00:13:14.060 --> 00:13:22.830
Raised to it in the year nine hundred and eight, while still a child, he was a caliph tossed about throughout his reign among commanders, viziers and palace factions.

132
00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:27.760
The treasury was empty, the army preoccupied with mutinies over pay.

133
00:13:27.760 --> 00:13:33.330
One of the caliph's official titles was guardian of the Sanctuary and of the pilgrimage road.

134
00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:39.220
After the morning of the year nine hundred and thirty, that title rang hollow wherever it was spoken.

135
00:13:39.220 --> 00:13:42.070
There was little that could be done.

136
00:13:42.070 --> 00:13:49.720
To mount a military campaign meant hunting the Qarmatians down in their own desert, and armies that had tried this before had been routed.

137
00:13:49.720 --> 00:13:52.730
So what remained was diplomacy and money.

138
00:13:52.730 --> 00:13:57.820
Envoys were sent to secure the stone's return; vast sums were offered.

139
00:13:57.820 --> 00:14:00.670
Abu Tahir refused them all.

140
00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:10.560
What he held in his hands was worth more than any amount of gold that could be offered, because the legitimacy of the side offering that gold rested on precisely that corner where the stone had sat.

141
00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:14.210
The outrage did not come from Baghdad alone.

142
00:14:14.210 --> 00:14:19.620
The event provoked fury throughout the Islamic world, across sectarian lines.

143
00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:29.350
In North Africa, the Fatimid caliph Ubayd Allah al-Mahdi wrote letters full of reproach to the Qarmatians of Bahrain, demanding the stone's return.

144
00:14:29.350 --> 00:14:37.320
This detail matters: the Qarmatians are often lumped together with the Fatimids, yet the raid on Mecca was a catastrophe for the Fatimids too.

145
00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:47.690
A dynasty trying to present itself as the rightful leadership of Islam gained nothing whatever from being charged with the plundering of the Kaaba by a movement sprung from the same root as itself.

146
00:14:47.690 --> 00:14:51.820
The gravest consequence, though, was felt on the roads.

147
00:14:51.820 --> 00:14:55.150
For years, the pilgrimage could not be made.

148
00:14:55.150 --> 00:15:02.640
The great caravans setting out from Iraq, those crowded convoys travelling by way of Kufa and Medina, came to a halt.

149
00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:10.770
To send out a pilgrim caravan meant putting thousands of people within range of Qarmatian cavalry, and no one wanted to take that responsibility.

150
00:15:10.770 --> 00:15:16.500
For years, only small groups reached Mecca, those willing to accept the risk themselves.

151
00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:25.910
Finally, in the year nine hundred and thirty-nine, an agreement was struck: the Qarmatians would permit the pilgrim caravans to pass in return for a payment made to them.

152
00:15:25.910 --> 00:15:31.800
The road to the holy city was reopened by a toll paid to the very people who had plundered it.

153
00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:36.090
That cut deeper into Baghdad's prestige than the loss of the stone.

154
00:15:36.090 --> 00:15:39.260
Al-Muqtadir never saw this solution.

155
00:15:39.260 --> 00:15:44.350
In the year nine hundred and thirty-two he was killed in a clash with his own soldiers.

156
00:15:44.350 --> 00:15:52.320
He went to his grave as a man who had held the caliphate for twenty-five years and had been unable to withstand the greatest humiliation of his age.

157
00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:56.130
Meanwhile, in Mecca, life went on in a strange way.

158
00:15:56.130 --> 00:15:59.460
After the agreement, the pilgrims returned.

159
00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:01.510
The circuits were performed.

160
00:16:01.510 --> 00:16:05.640
But in the corner where each circuit began, there was no stone.

161
00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:11.690
People turned towards that empty hollow, raised a hand and saluted the place, then began to walk.

162
00:16:11.690 --> 00:16:19.180
In the place of the sacred object stood the object's absence, and still people stopped at the same point and made the same gesture.

163
00:16:19.180 --> 00:16:23.630
This detail reveals the greatest error in Abu Tahir's calculation.

164
00:16:23.630 --> 00:16:26.320
No one went to Hajar.

165
00:16:26.320 --> 00:16:33.330
The stone sat in his mosque, but not a single person counted that mosque a place of pilgrimage; no one changed direction.

166
00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:36.980
The object had moved; the sanctity had not.

167
00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:43.830
What people sought was not the stone, but the place where the stone belonged and the meaning attached to that place.

168
00:16:43.830 --> 00:16:48.600
The pilgrimage was made without the stone, and it went on being the pilgrimage.

169
00:16:48.600 --> 00:16:52.090
Abu Tahir never saw the stone go back.

170
00:16:52.090 --> 00:16:57.180
He died of smallpox in nine forty-four; he was about thirty-eight at the time.

171
00:16:57.180 --> 00:17:04.510
Pious sources prefer to describe his death in the grip of a hideous disease, rotting away, as a punishment sent from heaven.

172
00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:09.440
The more measured records we have point to an ordinary epidemic of the period.

173
00:17:09.440 --> 00:17:19.170
Either way, the young leader who had still been in his mid-twenties when he sacked Mecca was gone before he reached forty, and he left behind a stone that even he had not known what to do with.

174
00:17:19.170 --> 00:17:23.620
Authority passed to his brothers and to the council descended from Abu Sa'id.

175
00:17:23.620 --> 00:17:31.270
And that council, unlike his older brother, saw that the thing in their hands was not a source of power but a burden too heavy to carry.

176
00:17:31.270 --> 00:17:36.040
In the year nine fifty-one, a sack was left in the great mosque of Kufa.

177
00:17:36.040 --> 00:17:39.370
Beside it lay a note saying what was inside.

178
00:17:39.370 --> 00:17:49.100
That one short sentence, as the sources report it, holds all the stubbornness of twenty-one years: we took it by a command, and by a command we have brought it back.

179
00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:54.670
No apology, no justification, no mention of a redeemer.

180
00:17:54.670 --> 00:17:58.320
Only the dry notice of a hand that gives as it took.

181
00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:04.690
What brought the Qarmatians to this point is not precisely known; the sources offer different reasons.

182
00:18:04.690 --> 00:18:10.100
By one account, a large sum was paid in a bargain struck with the government in Baghdad.

183
00:18:10.100 --> 00:18:18.390
By another, holding the stone no longer carried any political return at all: the toll taken from the pilgrimage road was secure in any case,

184
00:18:18.390 --> 00:18:23.480
while the stone did nothing but keep the hatred of the whole Islamic world alive.

185
00:18:23.480 --> 00:18:34.010
For a movement that had lost its old fervour even among its own people after the upheaval of nine thirty-one, the empty space in the corner of the Kaaba was no longer a card to play, but a weight.

186
00:18:34.010 --> 00:18:37.820
It was no accident that the stone was left in Kufa.

187
00:18:37.820 --> 00:18:44.670
The city was the soil in which the movement had been born, and at the same time a place Baghdad could easily reach.

188
00:18:44.670 --> 00:18:49.920
The sack was taken up, its contents were authenticated, and it was sent to Mecca.

189
00:18:49.920 --> 00:18:59.170
Tradition attaches a story of its own to that moment of verification: the stone, it is said, would not sink when thrown into water and would not grow hot in fire,

190
00:18:59.170 --> 00:19:03.300
and it was by this test that the returned fragments were known to be genuine.

191
00:19:03.300 --> 00:19:15.750
A story of the same kind travels along the road as well; the camel that carried the stone to Hajar is said to have perished on the way, while the camel that brought it back grew stronger with every mile.

192
00:19:15.750 --> 00:19:25.960
These tales were made in order to bear the sheer size of what had happened; they are not historical documents, but they do show how deep a mark those twenty-one years had left in people's minds.

193
00:19:25.960 --> 00:19:30.090
The stone was set back in place in nine fifty-one.

194
00:19:30.090 --> 00:19:32.940
The hollow in the corner was closed.

195
00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:37.390
But what was closed was not the same as what had been prised out in nine thirty.

196
00:19:37.390 --> 00:19:40.240
It had come back in pieces.

197
00:19:40.240 --> 00:19:48.530
Setting it in place meant holding those pieces together; it was mounted inside a silver frame, and the band that carried it was repaired and renewed.

198
00:19:48.530 --> 00:19:56.980
The silver ring you can see today in the eastern corner of the Kaaba, and the few dark fragments inside it, are the direct trace of that journey.

199
00:19:56.980 --> 00:20:05.430
Over the centuries that frame has been mended many times, replaced, remade; but its function, to hold the stone together, has never changed.

200
00:20:05.430 --> 00:20:12.280
One of the most touched objects in the world has stood from that day to this in a broken state, dependent on a frame.

201
00:20:12.280 --> 00:20:16.570
The Qarmatian state outlived the stone, but it never recovered.

202
00:20:16.570 --> 00:20:25.500
On the toll it took from the pilgrimage road, the tribute it gathered from the surrounding regions and the date groves of al-Ahsa, it stayed on its feet for another century.

203
00:20:25.500 --> 00:20:37.310
That city with no taxes and free-running mills, which Nasir Khusraw described with such admiration in ten fifty-one, was in truth a city living on its past: the awaited redeemer had not come,

204
00:20:37.310 --> 00:20:45.280
people still remembered what the one said to have come in nine thirty-one had amounted to, and nobody dared make the same claim a second time.

205
00:20:45.280 --> 00:20:49.250
Their rule in the region came to an end in ten sixty-seven.

206
00:20:49.250 --> 00:20:58.180
Today, on that coast of eastern Arabia, what remains of a state that once minted its own coinage is a few lines of record and the memory of a stone's absence.

207
00:20:58.180 --> 00:21:01.990
The real question that remains is where Abu Tahir was wrong.

208
00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:04.840
He thought holiness lay in the object.

209
00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:13.770
He imagined that if he took the stone, the centre would move with it, that people would turn in a new direction, that the address of the thing called pilgrimage would become Hajar.

210
00:21:13.770 --> 00:21:21.740
The stone stayed in his hands for twenty-one years, and in all those twenty-one years not a single pilgrim caravan went to Hajar.

211
00:21:21.740 --> 00:21:30.510
Even in the years when the stone was gone, pilgrims walked to Mecca, stood before the empty hollow, raised a hand and began the circling.

212
00:21:30.510 --> 00:21:39.280
What made the stone portable was physics; what made it sacred was the shared orientation of a community, and that was not something you could load onto a camel.

213
00:21:39.280 --> 00:21:44.530
Anyone who looks at the silver frame in that corner today sees more than an old stone.

214
00:21:44.530 --> 00:21:55.060
The ring that holds the fragments together is the record of a morning on which an empire failed to protect its most sacred object, of a well filling with bodies, of caravans that for years could not set out,

215
00:21:55.060 --> 00:21:58.870
and of one silent sentence left in Kufa with a sack.

216
00:21:58.870 --> 00:22:02.360
The object came back, broken.

217
00:22:02.360 --> 00:22:06.810
Of the state built by the men who took it, not even a frame remains.

218
00:22:06.810 --> 00:22:10.940
That brings us to the end of today's episode of Nöron Tarih.

219
00:22:10.940 --> 00:22:13.630
Until we meet again in a new story.
